WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:07.080
'Tis the season for Time Warner, owner of Turner Broadcasting, owner of the Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer pre-1986 film library,

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:11.180
to pump your household full of A Christmas Story for 24 hours straight.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:17.420
- [SLEIGH BELLS]
- Let's talk about how A Christmas Story is technological nostalgia yearning for a prelapsarian world—

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:18.920
by definition fictional—

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:23.480
encapsulated in material objects as signifier of the Age of Innocence,

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:26.340
a false memory of a time that never truly existed,

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.680
implanted in the collective ideoform through corporate repetition.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:31.760
I love Christmas so much.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:35.460
[SLEIGH BELLS SLOW IN PITCH, STOP]

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:42.480
♪♪

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:48.360
I don't think A Christmas Story is a particularly good movie, and I've honestly never liked it.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:51.040
As a child, I found it boring and obnoxious.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:54.440
My feelings towards it have softened a bit watching it as an adult;

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:57.420
I can appreciate the craft now, better than I could as a kid,

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:03.100
and in particular, I think Peter Billingsley is quite talented as a physical actor, especially for a child actor.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:09.480
But, overall, seeing it through adult eyes hasn't actually improved my opinion of the movie

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.800
as much as it has shifted the nature of what I dislike about it.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.100
A Christmas Story, advertising itself as

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:21.640
"a tribute to the Original Traditional, One-Hundred Percent, Red-Blooded, Two-Fisted, All-American Christmas",

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:29.840
was released in 1983 to lukewarm reception before finding an audience through the 80s and 90s in increasingly frequent televised broadcasts,

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:35.720
escalating to the point that in 1997 Turner Broadcasting, shortly after being bought out by Time Warner,

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:41.100
began airing a 24-hour marathon over Christmas Eve and Christmas Day on TBS.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:48.960
The film consists of Ralphie as an adult recounting what he frames as a particularly memorable Christmas from his early 1940s childhood,

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.600
a technique that would get recycled in similar nostalgia-driven properties The Wonder Years

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:57.840
and the very, very strange Tom Hanks film Radio Flyer.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:02.180
"I'm going to get worse."

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.820
"Why is this happening to Bobby and not to me?"

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:10.460
"Because Bobby cannot fight back."

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.480
Damn, I've gotta talk about Radio Flyer someday.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:16.740
The story here plays out in a series of loose vignettes,

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:23.880
mostly centering around 9-year-old Ralphie's desire to get a Red Ryder Carbine Action 200-shot Range Model Air Rifle for Christmas,

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:28.660
his attempts to persuade the adults around him, and various other incidental episodes.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:30.740
Cynically, the film's version of the

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:35.220
"Original, Traditional, One-Hundred Percent, Red-Blooded, Two-Fisted, All-American Christmas"

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:38.780
manifests largely as technological nostalgia.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:45.180
The film's eye is, like Ralphie, particularly obsessed with material things and how they used to be.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:54.120
The children in the opening montage stand outside Higby's department store looking in wonder at what the adult Ralphie narrates as "mechanized electronic joy"—

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.700
a montage of an animatronic Ms. Claus,

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.000
electric trains puttering along on their tracks,

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:02.120
and wind-up tin tanks that drive themselves.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:08.760
Several scenes with Ralphie's old man hinge their humor on his father's combat with the household furnace and electrical systems,

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.540
with one gag in particular relying on knowing

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.560
what this device is and how it works.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:20.240
Ralphie and his brother crowd around the radio to listen to Little Orphan Annie serials.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:24.240
In a particularly memorable scene, endlessly referenced by later works,

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.780
Ralphie finally receives his Little Orphan Annie secret decoder pin,

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:31.540
ecstatic to be a part of the privileged Little Orphan Annie inner circle,

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:35.160
but comes to the sad realization that he's been sold a bill of goods,

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.000
as the evening's secret message decodes to

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.600
"Be sure to drink your Ovaltine," a plug for the show's sponsor.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:45.460
As Ralphie says, "all that for a crummy commercial."

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:48.340
And Ovaltine &amp; Little Orphan Annie aren't alone;

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:56.940
Oldsmobile, Raggedy Ann, Red Ryder, Radio Flyer, The Wizard of Oz, Mickey Mouse, Lux, Palmolive, and Oxydol are all placed or referenced.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:01.940
The "Original, Traditional, One-Hundred Percent, Red-Blooded, Two-Fisted, All-American Christmas"

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:06.600
is unabashedly a Christmas of things and brands.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:12.760
The oddity of this particular brand of nostalgia is that it makes A Christmas Story a movie about kids,

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:15.760
but only nominally *for* kids.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:21.860
The focus is not on the world as children perceive it, but on the childhood world remembered by adults.

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:25.060
The iconography, the references, the physical space—

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:29.580
the film doesn't resemble the world children exist in, and it never did.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:36.380
Already by 1983, a 9-year-old had no point of reference for an extended joke about the furnace damper.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:42.240
It is a movie nebulously set in a very distinct time, that explicitly looks with fondness

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:47.180
on the materials of a childhood already 40 years distant at the time of its construction.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:54.040
And that fact, that it is a period piece, certainly does help to make the film feel older than it really is,

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:58.280
feel like it is part of some tradition that has survived generations.

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:03.620
This all ties into why I think the film did poorly in theatres, but better on television.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:07.000
Television adds to the sleight of hand, disguises age.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:13.300
Movies that were on TV in the 80s and 90s were, by definition, no longer brand new.

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:17.160
It is structurally poorly suited for watching in a theater anyway,

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:23.080
but perfectly suited for playing in the background while everyone bustles around the house doing holiday stuff.

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:30.220
The film drones along while everyone chatters until someone notices the screen and says "Hey, everyone, here's the best part,"

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:36.660
and then recites their favorite joke in rough sync with the film before going back to the snack table to get more punch.

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:42.260
The fact that the movie is so structuralist makes it somewhat perfect for TNT's 24-hour looping marathon;

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:43.980
it just kind of ends.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:51.020
At the end of the film, the neighbor's dogs eat the dinner turkey, and the family ends up instead at the Bo Ling &amp; Sons Chop Suey Palace Co.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:56.200
for a painfully long joke revolving around Chinese accents and the song Deck the Halls.

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:59.980
♪ "Deck the harrs with boughs of horry"

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:04.020
♪ "Fa ra ra ra ra, ra ra ra ra"

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:06.560
♪ "Fa ra ra ra ra, ra ra ra ra"

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:11.480
After their dinner of duck, that's pretty much it.

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:17.700
The family tucks away for the night, Ralphie and Randy clutching their things, and the credits roll.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:27.180
Unlike most holiday fare, there's no moral, no lessons, no affirmation of human goodness or appeals to the better, softer nature of mankind.

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:31.660
It is a story about a child who gets a toy, and little more.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:36.420
It's about that one year when a lot happened, none of it meant anything...

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:38.580
be sure to drink your Ovaltine.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:44.380
I don't think the movie is a particularly good movie, but is a darkly perfect match for the medium of television;

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:52.400
a handful of memorable scenes, competently executed, strung together in a way that actually improves more the less you pay attention.

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:57.840
However, I don't think the medium change alone tells the whole story of the film's reassessment and rise in popularity.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:06.160
The shift from the big screen to the small, the mode of engagement changing from being confined in a theater to drifting in and out between chores—

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:10.200
all of this is substantial, but it isn't the entire picture.

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:16.000
Autobiographically, I was actually shocked when I realised this movie came out after I was born.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:21.780
It had, in my mind as a child, always been there, and loved and quoted as it was by my parents,

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:26.620
in some level of my consciousness I had assumed it was as old as they were,

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:30.740
from the nebulous "before-time" preceding my existence.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:37.620
It is instead a little younger than me, and merely laser-focused on my parents and their memories of childhood,

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:41.900
as the 50s in rural Canada were not so different from the urban 40s.

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:47.000
The movie's rise in popularity, however, doesn't explode until the late 90s;

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:51.200
until people who were young enough to see A Christmas Story as children

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:56.000
were old enough to associate the movie directly with their own childhoods,

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:01.000
old enough for the movie to become their own object of nostalgia.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:08.560
It should be noted too that the conditions for that association are, in their own way, as artificial as the products Ralphie covets.

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:12.020
In 1985, Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer was purchased by Ted Turner,

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:18.760
who was looking to secure their substantial back catalogue of films as held assets for his WTBS SuperStation.

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:25.520
The following year, Turner sold off every component part of MGM except the pre-1986 film library.

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:28.980
This gave Turner a huge library of films to exploit for broadcast,

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:35.340
as Turner Entertainment could license the MGM films to Turner Broadcasting for functionally nothing.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:39.020
The proliferation of A Christmas Story, in addition to a number of other films,

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:43.380
has as much to do with the cost for broadcasters to license them

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:45.860
as it does their actual popularity.

00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:53.840
This is why actually really popular and successful Christmas movies like Home Alone, Elf and The Santa Clause are only occasionally on broadcast,

00:08:53.840 --> 00:09:03.100
while the objectively kinda bad Rankin-Bass Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer has aired more or less uninterrupted since 1964.

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:07.400
So, a movie that saw middling reception with its release in 1983

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:13.020
gained new life in 1986 as the functionally free holiday filler for Turner Broadcasting,

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:18.900
but didn't really gain true popularity until the maturation of a generation of children who had seen it as children.

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:23.200
And those people had seen it as children because it was on TV,

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:26.640
where it was a filler movie that stations could readily license,

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:31.680
and this synergistic surge in popularity was then capitalized on by TBS,

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:36.440
who could afford to air the movie as many times as they wanted, because they owned it.

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:44.080
The "tradition" of A Christmas Story, its true roots as a phenomenon of the season, the history of the 24-hour marathon

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:47.160
isn't a deep-rooted cultural practice;

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:50.380
it's a corporate stunt that's younger than Men in Black.

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:51.000
"WOO!"

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:56.260
Childhood nostalgia is a tricky thing, and should always be handled with some scepticism.

00:09:56.260 --> 00:10:02.980
Children by and large lack the means &amp; capacity to seek out, to curate, to filter.

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:07.900
In the 80s, you watched what you did because that's what was on TV.

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:12.320
Whether it was the decision of our parents or the person doing the scheduling at the network,

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:15.920
our childhoods were programmed for us.

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:21.400
It is altogether fitting that A Christmas Story, a story of nostalgia for things,

00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:26.540
wasn't able to succeed until the film itself became the object of nostalgia,

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:30.560
when it could become the self-contained symbol of childhood Christmases;

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:34.120
the evocation of sitting around a 12-inch tube television,

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:37.780
watching movies and running to the bathroom during commercial breaks.

00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:38.700
But...

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:44.080
to me, I'm Ralphie, sitting around the radio scratching away with my decoder pin,

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:50.140
only to realize that the message at the core of my childhood entertainment, well...

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:52.820
It's just a crummy commercial.

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:57.460
[SLEIGH BELLS]

